-- `number` is not a Saule type. It existed only as an internal sentinel in
-- the native signature registry meaning "integer or float accepted", and it
-- never worked the other way: a `number` value was assignable to neither
-- `integer` nor `float`, so anything annotated with it was unusable.
--
-- Each line below must produce exactly ONE diagnostic — the "not a type"
-- one. A rejected annotation is dropped rather than used to check the rest
-- of the binding, because using it produced a second error phrased as if
-- `number` were a type: "mark the type nullable with `?` (e.g. `number?`)",
-- advice that cannot be followed. The same held for `function`.

local uninitialized: number
local annotated: number = 5
local element: table<number> = {}
println(uninitialized, annotated, element)
